In this lesson, we'll look at how to list every possible outcome of a two-step chance experiment so nothing gets missed. First, pick a representation that suits you. You could write a simple list of ordered pairs, like head-head, head-tail, tail-head, tail-tail for two coin tosses. Or you could build a little table where the rows show what happens on step one and the columns show step two, and every cell holds one full outcome. A tree diagram works really well too, because each branch is one possible result at one step, and every leaf at the end is a full outcome. Now, counting is the easy part. If the first step has m outcomes and the second has n, the whole experiment has m times n outcomes. Two fair coins give two times two, that's four. Two dice give six times six, that's thirty-six. And finally, with a fair tree, the probability of any leaf is just the product of the probabilities along its branches, and all those leaf probabilities always add up to one. Now let's see it in action.